357
00:13:02,040 --> 00:13:05,000
then that enjoyment gets counted as a social gain.

358
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:06,840
But this doesn't mean an additional outcome.

359
00:13:06,840 --> 00:13:07,880
Why?

360
00:13:07,880 --> 00:13:10,440
This is a case where voters get the full psychological benefit

361
00:13:10,440 --> 00:13:13,880
of their rationality, but they pay only an infinitesimal

362
00:13:13,880 --> 00:13:15,880
fraction of the cost.

363
00:13:15,880 --> 00:13:18,880
So basically, each voter is subconsciously thinking,

364
00:13:18,880 --> 00:13:20,300
I can believe whatever I feel like,

365
00:13:20,300 --> 00:13:21,880
and it won't really change the outcome.

366
00:13:21,880 --> 00:13:24,040
Just like when an environmentalist drives.

367
00:13:24,040 --> 00:13:25,440
Subconsciously saying, yeah, well, it's

368
00:13:25,440 --> 00:13:27,180
really a shame that we got global warming,

369
00:13:27,180 --> 00:13:30,440
but I need to go and get to Marshalls to buy some clothes.

370
00:13:30,440 --> 00:13:33,200
So that's just what's going to happen.

371
00:13:33,200 --> 00:13:37,680
Now, if enough voters rely on systematically biased beliefs

372
00:13:37,680 --> 00:13:39,800
to decide how to vote, then disastrous policies

373
00:13:39,800 --> 00:13:40,840
can be adopted.

374
00:13:40,840 --> 00:13:43,960
Then you might say, see, there is a cost.

375
00:13:43,960 --> 00:13:46,600
But remember, is there a marginal cost?

376
00:13:46,600 --> 00:13:49,480
Like, suppose you voted against Hitler in 1933.

377
00:13:49,480 --> 00:13:51,560
What did you get?

378
00:13:51,560 --> 00:13:52,880
Hitler.

379
00:13:52,880 --> 00:13:55,760
So what good did it actually do for you

380
00:13:55,760 --> 00:13:57,760
